 Good Wednesday afternoon. Today's newsletter, edited by Alex Fitzpatrick, is 668 words, a 2½-min. read. Thanks to Sheryl Miller for copy editing . 

 ⚡️ Situational awareness: Democrats took control of the Texas state House after historic wins for governor and U.S. Senate in yesterday's elections.

 Republicans held control since 2002. The win for Democrats was part of a series of sweeping gains in state governments for the party. Go deeper . 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 1 big thing: Vance blames war for GOP&#x27;s catastrophic night 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Photo illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios. Photos: Brandon Bell and U.S. Navy via Getty Images Opposition to the Iran war fueled the Democrats' "blue tsunami," Vice President Vance argued in a surprisingly blunt midterms postmortem today, Axios' Herb Scribner reports. 

 Vance's comments effectively kick off the 2028 election cycle, with the vice president distancing himself from Trump's highly unpopular war. 🤳 Vance wrote on X : "The reason we lost is the war. The president himself said so last night."

 "If you'd like to argue it was worth it, that losing the Senate for the next 6 years was worth it, then do so. Argue it was a worthwhile cost to pay. Agree or disagree, that's at least honest." Vance argued that Republicans need to do four things to win voters back: End the Iran war, focus on inflation, "put America First," and "change our relationship to Israel."

 He wrote: "To the people who are just pissed at the results, you should be. Let's take that anger and get back to the work of saving our country." ⛽️ Trump said this afternoon that he agrees that the war "drove the gasoline up a little bit, short term."

 "But at the same time, if I had to do it again, I'd have to do it again because long term, it's the only answer," he added at an impromptu White House ballroom event. "You have no choice. You have to do it. You can't let Iran have a nuclear weapon." The intrigue: Donald Trump Jr. responded to Vance's post: "Every word of this is 💯💯💯."

 Neither the White House nor the vice president's office responded to Axios' requests for comment.

